# A committed TEMPLATE of .github/c2-serving-job.env for the tp4/prefix-tiers development window (the preconverted GDN checkpoints and the host KV tier; scripts/ci/c2_serving_job.py parses it):
# copy it over .github/c2-serving-job.env on a throwaway commit of tp4/prefix-tiers and push a tag experiment/c2-serving-vN. Nothing here names a rig, a card, a host or a registry: the cards are
# resolved at run time. The jobs are templates only: no tag is pushed from this branch by its author. ORDER.txt has the order, the stop rules, the minutes and the read rules.
# ONE IMAGE FOR THE WHOLE WINDOW: C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-prefix-tiers-1 is what B0 builds from the pushed tp4/prefix-tiers head (sed -i 's/tp4-prefix-tiers-1/<tag>/' *.env); every later job serves that one
# image. A commit of a file the image carries needs a new B0 build and a new tag in every template. The cards are under development: this window never stops, starts or hands back the node agent.
# T2 (STOP): the RETURNING-SESSION SMOKE that forces the eviction, then the reuse (c2_prefix_gate.py --plan tier-returning, scenario tier_returning) on the audited tier profile (c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-tier-audit), audits on, no baseline. Three salted sessions are built to ~56k tokens
# (two growth turns each, every one a hit on the one before), then FRESH-SALT FLOODS totalling the whole KV pool plus one flood (~24 floods of 56k at the production pool of 19,968 blocks; the pool is read from vLLM's 'GPU KV cache size' line) so that
# every cached block of the sessions leaves the device pool, spilled to host RAM by the scheduler graft; then each session's next turn as a cold/hit pair: the hit's attention-KV pages come back from the tier BYTE FOR BYTE (the digest of every block is
# checked at restore, QWEN_PREFIX_HOST_TIER_VERIFY=all, and each restored block is read back from the device and compared with the bytes written, QWEN_PREFIX_HOST_TIER_AUDIT=1) and its answer must equal the cold twin's, every output token. Then one more
# turn on a restored session, and the TIER'S OWN KILL SWITCH: the gate writes the tier's kill-switch file inside the container, the tier latches off and drops its records, the next turn is still exact. A tenant never meets another's pages by construction
# (the blocks are keyed by the salted block hash); the cold twin, a fresh salt of the same prompt, is that probe: it must not restore.
# READ: the plan verdict PASS; 'after the flood (L, Q, oracle Q, blocks restored from host RAM)' with a restore on every session and Q equal to the oracle's (a Q under the oracle with no tier loss counter is a FAIL, with one a note); tier_spill_blocks,
# tier_restore_requests, tier_restore_blocks, tier_audit_reads and tier_digest_checks all non-zero; tier_spill_failures, tier_restore_failures, tier_restore_refused_digest, tier_digest_failures and tier_audit_mismatches all ZERO; the
# '[PREFIX-AUDIT-CKPT]' lines equal; the kill switch: 'host tier latched off', tier_latched=1, the file removed, the latched turn exact; no pins held, no commit mismatch; 'host tier spill' and 'host tier restore' lines give the real rates
# (bytes and ms) of the device reads and writes; the 'tier rss=' lines the host RSS growth (the slab). NOT_EXERCISED when the flood left the sessions on the device (restore_requests 0): then the pool figure or the flood size is the finding.
# Every request of the arm is sent alone, so the multi-user SDPA audit's passing-line rule is not asked of it (see T1; c2_prefix_gate.sdpa_audit_optional). It does not exercise a restore beside OTHER seats'
# decodes: that is a packed-round question this plan does not ask yet. Unqualified. Duration (estimate): 150 minutes plus the boot (the floods are the cost: a pool of cold prefills). Stop rule.
C2_CARDS=quad
C2_ACTIONS=reset prefix
C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-prefix-tiers-1
C2_PREFIX_PLAN=tier-returning
C2_PREFIX_PROFILE=c2-packed-tp4-8x262k-ship-prefix-levern-w2-er-tier-audit
C2_PREFIX_BASELINE=none
C2_BOX_MINUTES=190
